{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And he took the calf that they had made.</b> There was no need to say ״that they had made.״ But perhaps it is because the calf had two aspects: on the side of its head it resembled a calf, and on the side of its tail it resembled a donkey, as stated in the words of the Sages of blessed memory (Zohar vol. 2 192a). Therefore it would not suffice to say ״and he took the calf״ alone, for then the mistaken person might err and think that he burned only the calf side of it and not the donkey side. Therefore it says <b>that they had made</b>, to include the entire entity, even the donkey portion." "<b>Further,</b> it intends along the lines of the verse (Jeremiah 2:19) ״תְּיַסְּרֵךְ רָעָתֵךְ״, ״וְתַמְּגֶנּוּ״ etc. (Isaiah 64:5) — that the sin itself punishes its doer, for the grasp of evil is itself what destroys its lovers and the haters of holiness. And that is what it says: <b>that they had made</b> … ״and he made the children of Israel drink it.״ And this matter will be a clarifying lesson to open blind eyes, to hate the aspect of evil and to love the level of holiness — love of the good insofar as it is good." "<b>Further,</b> or it may be that it intends to allude to the aspect of evil that the two sorcerers made, through whose sorcery the calf emerged and would speak, saying ״אֵלֶּה אֱלֹהֶיךָ״ — that even that he took. For from the aspect of the dominion of holiness over the kelipah, it is fitting to use the language of ״taking״ — that he would have dominion over it so that it could not turn away from before him until he did with it as seemed good in his eyes. And behold, before you is the account of the Men of the Great Assembly (Yoma 69b), who took the evil impulse of idolatry and also of forbidden relations and sought to destroy it. And from this you will understand that likewise Moses had dominion over the like of this, to take it and to consume it with a devouring fire. And that is what it says <b>and he burned in the fire</b> — that it is far more fitting to use the language of burning with respect to the aspect of the evil spirit within the calf than to say, as the plain sense of the verse would have it, that it refers to the body of the calf, which is the gold. For gold is not consumed by fire unless one posits a miracle or an intermediary agent, but the aspect of the evil spirit within it is far more fitting for the language of burning. And this is the sod of (Isaiah 11:4) ״וּבְרוּחַ שְׂפָתָיו יָמִית רָשָׁע״."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:36:12.074265358Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}